Now, to answer your questions.  No the rule doesn't apply to viewing "nude  
images" in the Library -- it is a double standard on policy but not on  
implementation.  First, the situation is different at the library as viewing  
"nude images" on computers is not supported (at this time) so the situation,  
from the start is different.  Second, viewing of materials is generally  
private, i.e, the vcr/tcs are in carrels, the microfish/microfilm readers  
are in carrels.  Basically, what the person is viewing/reading, etc, is not  
readily visible to the casual observer (which is important!) and thus few if  
any are actually offended...

However, in our computing lab, anything viewed on the computer monitors is  
readily visible to anyone who walks by, sits on the workstation next to the  
person, etc.  Our workstations are _not_ in carrels, they are on computer  
tables and in rows.  We also use the lab to teach computer courses, thus the  
computers are arranged in a way so that every student at a computer can see  
the front of the room.  We are not able to simply turn the computers around  
so that they face the wall and keep the contents of the screen away from  
public view...

So the difference, in essance, is public vs private viewing.  The library is  
set up for a more private atmosphere while our lab is set up for the most  
public as possible.  In any of our rooms (we have 5 large classrooms in our  
lab) you can see at minimum, 8 computer screens from anywhere in the room...


