	A better question might be if students are subject to charges
under UH's Sexual Harassment policy if they are seen reading a magazine
such as _Playboy_ or _Penthouse_ on "UH property". Could someone be
punished for reading the magazine on a bench during a break, or over a
meal in the cafeteria? Note this is a "public" setting, even more so
than a computer cluster - those tend to be rather isolated. Observers
could rather easily look over the reader's shoulder and see the dreaded
pictures. I would hope that the absurdity of a university disciplining
students for reading "offensive" magazines would be evident, but I
wouldn't count on it.

>Every sexual harassment rule I've ever read, requires a hearing. It
>also requires that someone actually be harassed.

	The way this works is intimidation. It's just too costly and
difficult for almost any student to fight a "sexual harassment" charge,
no matter how frivolous. It's a classic "chilling effect". What ends up
happening is people are threatened that they can give in, or see you in
"court" (more like the Star Chamber, though). Almost everyone in this
