If the University tries to stop that by seeking an obscenity ruling, then 
the University's legal counsel should be sacked.  "All they need to do is 
to get an obscenity ruling"?!  SHEESH.  That is *not* the easy way out.  
Any legal advisor worth poop ought to know that "reasonable time and 
place" requirements should prove sufficient for this case--unless, of 
course, the University regularly permits the showing of any available 
programming material on the large screen tv installed at the entrance for 
just that purpose.

The school can stop this because it isn't the place for such a display.  
I do recall, though, attending showings of X-rated films in University 
facilities.  Your school ought not to pick and choose which 
(legitimate--e.g., registered) student groups are permitted to use school 
facilities.  (Even private institutions ought to be pretty careful here.)

So, there: I've handed you an argument, though I've taken (a really 
silly) one away, too.  What can you make of the argument?  Can you show 
that reasonable time and place strictures restrict use of computer 
facilities as UH practice does?  How much is the computer lab relevantly 
like the entrance to the student union?  What *other* restrictions are 
there, having to do with reasonable time and place, concerning use of 
computer facilities?  Specifically, what other restrictions are there on 
the use of displays, having to do with resonable time and place?

