: We do have a procedure for handling sexual harassment complaints.  It is  
: provided to every student in the form of the University of Houston Student  
: Handbook (available to all students - actually give to every new student  
: when they enroll).  Here is one except that I find relevant:

: "... sexually harassing behavior may include, but is not limited to, the  
: following:  

[I've added the itemization--BU]

:	(1) unwelcome sexual flirtations, advances, or propositions; 
: 	(2) verbal remarks of a sexual nature (whether directed to an 
: individual or a group), including sexually explicit or offensive jokes; 
:	(3) graphic or degrading verbal or written comments of a sexual 
: nature about an individual or the individual's appearance; 
:	(4) any suggestive or unwelcome physical contact; or  
: 	(5) physical assault.  
: Sexual harassment is not limited by gender of either  
: party, nor by superior-subordinate relationships.
:    Failure to investigate allegations of sexual harassment or failure to  
: take timely corrective action is considered a violation of university Sexual  
: Harassment Policy.  The university is prepared to take preventative and  
: corrective action in cases of sexual harassment: individuals in such  
: misconduct are subject to appropriate disciplinary action."

Hmmmm.  Let's see how one would make the sexual harassment case to 
restrict use of computer terminals as UH does.  (It will turn out that 
the case must be made that the behavior is harasssing, though not 
included in the list of examples.)

Is display of "offensive" GIFs a violation of (1)?  Only if said display 
is a flirtation, an advance, or a proposition.  Since, as the situations 
have been described thus far, the display isn't really *directed* at any 
one, it isn't any of these, and it isn't excluded under (1).  (Carl made 
this point in a previous post, and I noted it above.)

Is it a violation of (2)?  Well, it isn't verbal.  But let's stretch the 
bounds of the rule for a moment.  Again, since not *directed* at any one, 
display of "offensive" GIFs doesn't violate (2).

(3)?  Let's say the GIFs are graphic.  Let's say (all for the purposes of 
argument) that they're degrading and of a sexual nature.  Under (3) they 
still have to be about *an individual* or *the individual's appearance.*  
So, unless they're supposed to relate to, say, Paul, (3) isn't violated.

(4)?  (5)?  No physical contact at issue, so these aren't violated.  
(Unless someone wants to make a very interesting case.)

So, if one wanted to make a sexual harassment case out of this, one would 
not be much aided by the behaviors itemized in the policy.

Hence, Paul raises the "hostile environment" theory.  I'm sure someone 
will correct me if I'm mistaken, but doesn't success under the hostile 
environment theory require that the allegedly harassing behavior be 
"pervasive" or something like that?  Unless the behavior pervades the 
environment, I don't see that it can make the overall environment hostile 
or, for that matter, welcoming.

The situation seems to me to be the following:  There appear to be two
theories under which the practices at UH might be justified.  One theory
appeals to reasonable time and place requirements placed on speech.  The
other appeals to the theory of creating a hostile working environment as a
form of sexual harassment.  Either case would take some making--just
gesturing in the general direction of a principle ought not to settle the
issue.  (Did I mention that reasonable time and place requirements have to
be content-neutral?) Further, even if such a case were to be made, it is
only a prima facie defense of UH practices, since it will remain an open
question whether the policy under discussion is the appropriate means of
achieving the desired end.  (For instance, UH is obliged to defer to free
speech by taking less intrusive, rather than more intrusive measures where
it may be justified in restricting speech.)

Although Paul frequently refers to "obscenity," and is not alone in this, 
nothing has yet shown that any of the material in question is obscene.  
Even if some of it is, UH should think long and hard about the 
qualifications as judges of obscenity of those in positions to enforce 
the policy.

More likely, the real root of the ban is a theory of "offense".  
(Highlighted, way, way, way above.)  The thinking is that material that 
offends ______ ought to be banned.  Although the motive might be noble, 
filling in the blank in a sensible way proves to be devilishly hard.  
Allowing that anything that offends *anyone* whatever is clearly too 
broad (though rules sometimes get written that way).  Such a rule allows 
the minority and those with very odd sensibilities to determine what 
everyone else sees, hears, reads, and so on.  (For my part, if I see that 
poster of the kid with spaghetti dumped on his head one more time ... )  
The usual tack is to appeal to that which offends "a reasonable person", 
but that just moves the problem.  Whose judgments do we accept concerning 
what counts?  Is a reasonable person relatively easy or relatively hard 
to offend?  Offended by prurient material?  Violence?  Overly sentimental 
stuff?  Same sex?  "Unnatural" positions?  Irreligious material?  What 
religions?  All?  Graphic portrayal of drug use?  Which drugs?  Portrayal 
of drunkenness?  Disresepct for elders?  Disrespect for a given race?  
Disrespect for a given operating system?  Hmmmm.  Hard to see.

OTOH, there are good reasons to leave offense principles aside 
altogether.  (I suspect this is where Carl usually gets into these 
debates, although not always in these terms.)  Appealing to a "harm 
principle" instead is a better bet.  (IMHO, IMHO!)  That's the point of the 
requirements in the UH sexual harassment policy that behavior be *directed* 
at someone.

Phew.  That should do for now.

