[MUNCH]

: 	The way this works is intimidation. It's just too costly and
: difficult for almost any student to fight a "sexual harassment" charge,
: no matter how frivolous. It's a classic "chilling effect". What ends up
: happening is people are threatened that they can give in, or see you in
: "court" (more like the Star Chamber, though). Almost everyone in this
: situation capitulates, the exceptions are very rare. If there's no
: disincentive to making accusations - if the worst that can happen is
: that the accuser is told they aren't sustained - this fosters a "what's
: there to lose?" mentality that can extend to the most trivial of incidents

It's handy to have a rule that bars frivolous prosecution--not to be 
invoked lightly, mind you, surely not merely for losing a case.  When I 
worked in the judicial office a BigStateU, the student code had a 
"catch-all" prohibition on activities that interfere with ordinary 
university activities, such as teaching, research, and administration.  
(That's a quick gloss, not a quotation.  The code also noted that mere 
police response, e.g., didn't constitute a violation.)

I recall one instance in which the provision was invoked over a frivolous 
prosecution.  Student A accused student B of an offense.  The office 
suspected that student A's accusation was, um, squirelly.  Student B 
threatened a counter-charge.  Come time for the hearing, student B 
dutifully appears, but student A is not present.  (Both had clear 
notice.)  The charge was not sustained against student B, naturally, but 
it appeared that student A had used the process to extract some measure 
of revenge on student B.  (I think B had to come back to campus in the 
summer, maybe.  It's been awhile.)  So, charges were brought against A 
for interfering with the judicial process.  (Both students left school, 
being, perhaps, a bit, err, unsure where life should take them.)

: >I think the U. of Houston may have stepped over the line.

