From caf-talk Caf Oct 27 01:05:14 1994
From: kadie@hal.cs.uiuc.edu (Carl M Kadie)
Newsgroups: alt.comp.acad-freedom.talk
Subject: Re: Indiana State University Proposed Policy
Date: 27 Oct 94 04:18:40 GMT
Message-ID: <kadie.783231520@hal.cs.uiuc.edu>

Summary: Participation in policy making seems very good. The policy
tries to offer protection for free expression, privacy, and due
process; but in every case the protection is so vague and has so many
loopholes that little real protection is provided. Most of the
loopholes could be fixed by integrating the policy better with
existing university policies and procedures.

Detals:

>The document has been reviewed by several
>University groups, and is now posted for public comment during
>the next XX weeks.  Following this period, it will be considered
>by the Faculty Senate and Student Government Association prior to
>submission to the Board of Trustees for approval.
[...]

This is excellent. (The call for participation could, however, be
improved by providing an email address for comments).

[...]
>
>                    INDIANA STATE UNIVERSITY
>                COMPUTING POLICIES AND PROCEDURES
>
>
[...]
>
>CONFIDENTIALITY
>In general, information stored on computers is considered
>confidential, unless the owner intentionally makes that
>information available to other groups or individuals.  Requests
>for disclosure of confidential information will be honored only
>when approved by University officials or when required by state
>or federal law.  Except when inappropriate, computer users will
>receive prior notice of such disclosures.  

Which "University officials"? This could mean the Chancellor or it
could mean any staff member. I suggest it be the same officials who
can authorize a dorm or office search.

[...]
>CENSORSHIP
>Free expression of ideas is central to the academic process.

This could be made more concrete by saying that computer media
and resources will be treating with the same principles that apply
to traditional University forums and media like the library and
student newspaers.

>  ISU
>computer system administrators will not remove any information
>from active individual accounts or from electronic bulletin
>boards maintained on them unless the appropriate system
>administrator determines that:
>
>     ~ The presence of the information involves illegality (e.g.,
>copyrighted material).

The University legal counsel is much better qualified that
sys admin to make legal judgments. 

>
>     ~ The information in some way endangers computing resources
>or the information of other users (e.g. a computer worm, virus,
>or other destructive program).
>
>     ~ The information is unrelated to or is inconsistent with
>the mission of the University.

This is much too vague. It gives sys admin authority to ban
anything. 

>
>     ~ The information involves the use of obscene or abusive
>language.

"Obscenity" is complex legal concept that few sys admin are likely
qualified to judge. "Abusive language" is generally protected by the
First Amendment and academic freedom. It should not in general be
subject to censorship. Does the University have a general speech code
that has not been struck down by the courts that authorizes the
University to censor "abusive speech". I don't think it does. The
computer should not be treating differently that other campus forums
and media.

The underlying problem with section is that it offers no due process:
no clear standard, no hearing, no appeal. As the American Library
Association's "Statement on Challenged Materials" says:

     Freedom of expression is protected by the Constitution of the United
     States, but constitutionally protected expression is often separated
     from unprotected expression only by a dim and uncertain line. The
     Constitution requires a procedure designed to focus searchingly on
     challenged expression before it can be suppressed. An adversary
     hearing is a part of this procedure.

>
>
>ILLEGAL USAGE
>Computing resources may not be used for illegal purposes.

This is the "law law": The rule that says it is a violation of
the rules to violate rules. I think it is redundant.

It could be made more useful by reminding users what other policies 
apply. For example:

[...]
>     ~ Harassment of other users.

What is the University's general policy on harassment? Instead of
creating a special, computer-only harassment policy, how about
just quoting the University's general policy.

[...]
> Any attempt to compromise the University computer security systems
>will not be tolerated.

This sounds like suspected violators will be subject to a summary
firing squad. It would be better to be more specific. For example:
"Any attempt to compromise the University computer security systems
will be subject to University displine."

[...]
>     ~ Intentional use of computer telecommunication facilities
>in ways that impede the computing activities of others.

This makes it illegal to use a computer terminal if someone is waiting
to use it. I suggest replacing "impede" with "substantially disrupt".
The phrase "substantially disrupt" is likely already used in the
Student Code and has case law defining it.

[...]
>     ~ Violation of network usage.

What is this?

>
>     ~ Violation of another user's privacy. 

This is too vague. Recall that the U. of Michigan's speech code
was struck down in part because it was too vague. Here is a quote
from the federal judge's opinion:

   [From _Doe v. University of Michigan_, 721 F. Supp. 852 (E.D. Mich. 1989)]

   C. Vagueness

y   Doe also urges that the policy be struck down on the grounds that
   it is impermissibly vague.  A statute is unconstitutionally vague
   when "men of common intelligence must necessarily guess at its
   meaning."  Broadrick, supra 413 U.S.  at 607, 93 S.Ct. at 2913.  A
   statute must give adequate warning of the conduct which is to be
   prohibited and must set out explicit standards for those who apply
   it.  Id.  "No one may be required at the peril of life, liberty or
   property to speculate as to the meaning of penal statutes.  All are
   entitled to be informed as to what the State commands or forbids."
   Lanzetta v. New Jersey, 306 U.S. 451, 453, 59 S.Ct. 618, 619, 83
   L.Ed. 888 (1939).  These considerations apply with particular force
   where the challenged statute acts to inhibit freedoms affirmatively
   protected by the constitution.  Smith v. Goguen, 415 U.S. 566, 573,
   94 S.Ct. 1242, 1247, 39 L.Ed.2d 605 (1974).  However, the chilling
   effect caused by an overly vague statute must be both real and
   substantial, Young v.  American Mini-Theatres, 427 U.S. 50, 96
   S.Ct. 2440, 49 L.Ed.2d 310 (1976), and a narrowing construction
   must be unavailable before a court will set it *867 aside, Screws
   v. United States, 325 U.S. 91, 98, 65 S.Ct. 1031, 1033, 89 L.Ed.
   1495 (1945).

[...]
>SANCTIONS 
>Violation of these policies will be dealt with seriously and will
>be subject to the disciplinary procedures of the University; in
>addition, the loss of some or all computing privileges may
>result.

This seems to say that users may lose all their computer access even
if the University disciplinary procedures finds them innocent!

I think it would be much better to say the result of University
disciplinary procedures may include loss computer access. Also, in
case of an emergency and with the approval of, say, the Chancellor,
users may be suspended from the computer pending due process. I
suggest the Chancellor because at many universities only he or she has
authority to impose summary suspension.


[...]

- Carl

