Subject: New ALA Intellectual Freedom Library Policies
Date: 29 Oct 1994 05:00:58 -0400
Message-ID: <38t30a$ore@eff.org>

Here is a summary of some changes and additions to American Library
Association recommended policies from the last year or so.  I think
many of these recommendations will be of interest to makers of
academic/public computer policy, too.  Highlights include:

   * [quote] "The American Library Association opposes any legislative or
      regulatory attempt to impose content restrictions .."
   * [paraphrase] Librarians should resist efforts to exclude material
     about any subject matter including sexual orientation.
   * Guidelines for development of policies regarding library use.
     [paraphrase] As much as possible use "existing legistation and
     law enforcement mechanism" rather than creating your own.
   * [paraphrase] Libraries and library meeting rooms, etc are
     "limited public forums".
   * A check list of 15 things to check for when creating a policy that
     restricts access.
   * A suggested procedure on how to handle police and other official
     requests to see a user's library records.

They can all be found via gopher. Try
   gopher -p1/library/ala/ala-x uic.edu
or by anoymous ftp to ftp.eff.org, directory pub/library.

>>> New statements of the Library Bill of Rights:

*** Economic Barriers to Information Access

Excerpt: "The American Library Association opposes the charging of user
fees for the provision of information by all libraries and
information services that receive their major support from public
funds. All information resources that are provided directly or
indirectly by the library, regardless of technology, format, or
methods of delivery, should be readily, equally and equitably
accessible to all library users."

and

"The American Library Association opposes any legislative or
regulatory attempt to impose content restrictions on library
resources, or to limit user access to information, as a condition
of funding for publicly supported libraries and information
services."

*** Access to Library Resources and Services Regardless of Gender of
Sexual Orientation

Excerpt: "The American Library Association stringently and unequivocally
maintains that libraries and librarians have an obligation to
resist efforts that systematically exclude materials dealing with
any subject matter, including gender, homosexuality, bisexuality,
lesbianism, heterosexuality, gay lifestyles, or any facet of sexual
orientation:"

*** Guidelines for the Development of Policies and Procedures
Regarding User Behavior and Library Use

Here is a paraphrase:
   1. As much as possible use "existing legistation and law
      enforcement mechanism" rather than creating your own.
   2. "[C]ite should cite statutes or ordinances upon which the authority
      to make those policies is based."
   3. Polices should not violate the Library Bill of Rights.
   4. Make policies reasonable and narrow.
   5. Have legal counsel check policy.
   6. "Common sense, reason and sensitivity should be used to resolve
      issues in a constructive and positive manner without escalation."
   7. Train staff. Include empathy training.
   8. Consider any policy that limits access very carefully.
      "[P]rovide a clear description of the behavior that is
      prohibited so that a reasonably intelligent person will
      have fair warning..."
     

>>>> Changed policies:

*** Guidelines for the Development and Implementation of Policies,
Regulations and Procedures Affecting Access to Library Materials,
Services and Facilities

Lots of lines changes, but the gist is the same.

"Thus, publicly supported libraries are governmental agencies
designated as limited public forums for access to information.
Libraries that make meeting rooms, exhibit spaces and/or bulletin
boards available for public use are also designated as limited public
forums for the exchange of information."

It includes a check list of 15 things to check for when creating a
policy that restricts access. For example:

"11.  must provide a means of appeal;

12.  must be reviewed regularly by the library's governing
     authority and by its legal counsel.

13.  must be communicated clearly and made available in an
     effective manner to all library users;"

>>> Policy on Confidentiality of Library Records

Some wording is new. Also, I believe this suggested procedure is new:

"When drafting local policies, libraries should consult with their
legal counsel to insure these policies are based upon and
consistent with applicable federal, state, and local law
concerning the confidentiality of library records, the disclosure
of public records, and the protection of individual privacy.

Suggested procedures include the following:

     1.  The library staff member receiving the request to
         examine or obtain information relating to circulation or
         other records identifying the names of library users,
         will immediately refer the person making the request to
         the responsible officer of the institution, who shall
         explain the confidentiality policy.

     2.  The director, upon receipt of such process, order, or
         subpoena, shall consult with the appropriate legal
         officer assigned to the institution to determine if such
         process, order, or subpoena is in good form and if there
         is a showing of good cause for its issuance.

     3.  If the process, order, or subpoena is not in proper form
         or if good cause has not been shown, insistence shall be
         made that such defects be cured before any records are
         released. (The legal process requiring the production
         of circulation or other library records shall ordinarily
         be in the form of subpoena "duces tecum" [bring your
         records] requiring the responsible officer to attend
         court or the taking of his/her deposition and may
         require him/her to bring along certain designated
         circulation or other specified records.)

     4.  Any threats or unauthorized demands (i.e., those not
         supported by a process, order, or subpoena) concerning
         circulation and other records identifying the names of
         library users shall be reported to the appropriate legal
         officer of the institution.

     5.  Any problems relating to the privacy of circulation and
         other records identifying the names of library users
         which are not provided for above shall be referred to
         the responsible officer."

>>> Statement on Meeting Rooms

The phrase "or group's ability to pay for that access" was added
to a line. Here is the result:

"It is inconsistent with Article V to restrict indirectly access to
library meeting rooms based on an individual's
or group's ability to pay for that access."


