*****************************************************************

Internet Censorship at Central Michigan University

*****************************************************************

     From the age of uniformity, from the age of solitude,
     from the age of Big Brother, from the age of
     doublethink - Greetings!

                      George Orwell (Eric Blair) (1903-1950)
                                 Nineteen Eighty-Four (1948)

*****************************************************************

Elliot Parker, Associate Professor, Department of Journalism,
Central Michigan University (CMU), censored the appended edition
of CONTRA CABAL by deleting it from listserv carr-l. The author,
Paul Trummel, a journalist/publisher for over 47 years, describes
in the censored text his 15-years experience as a professor of
communication. He also exposes widespread academic fraud that
will probably generate national concern. He intended the carr-l
post to give ethical investigative reporters the opportunity to
break a story that has received only listserv exposure. Like many
ethical journalists and academicians, Trummel has concerns about
the objectivity of self-investigation when exercising his
journalistic freedom. Consequently, to some extent, he relies=20
upon media colleagues to use the documented evidence that he has
accumulated.
     Parker, has imperiously decreed that CONTRA CABAL (with an
estimated 100,000 electronic readers) represents:

     ". . . a waste of time."

He censored it from carr-l because he considered that:

     "99% of the people here are going to read the first
      screen . . . and hit the delete key."

Then, the following day he denied any censorship of carr-l, thus:

     "This list belongs to you the users. . . . The list is
      self-moderated. . . . The list is not moderated."

Trummel emphatically contends that the decision whether to read
or delete material belongs to the subscriber and not to the
self-aggrandizing, devious Parker. Denis Cowan <brisnet.org.au>
aptly writes (in response to a previous attempt to censor CONTRA
CABAL from ccrnet <nova.edu>) that:

     "I believe it is Paul Trummel's right to post this message
      to any list."

     "I believe that it is my right to respond to it in whatever
      way I wish."

Parker's action epitomizes the insidious censorship that many
list managers and systems administrators now impose upon the
electronic media. They sanctify their own idea of political
correctness (totalitarianism) and try to justify it by claiming
to edit, moderate, or own lists. Regardless that no individual
can own Internet addresses, they call themselves "list owners"
instead of "list managers." They then try to unethically empower
themselves as censors. Parker's behavior seems even more
reprehensible when one considers that, as an associate professor
of journalism in a university department, he has implicitly sworn
to protect both academic and press freedom.
     The tragicomedy of Parker's behavior shows that the age of
the censor has not passed. Contrary to First Amendment tradition,
censorship has emerged again with a new generation of electronic
censors. Many of them consistently attempt to restrict
intellectual freedom by censoring Internet communications.
Despite academic hyperbole and denial, their attempts resemble
the censorship imposed at the French court by Turgot's Ancien
Regime. Conal Thomas' questions again become relevant:

not,

     "Does censorship exist?"

rather,

     "Under what kind of technological censorship does one now
      live?"

     The inexhaustible human (inhuman) capacity to find ways to
censor information becomes an ironic contrast to modern and
universal communication. Censorship curtails the communication of
truth and thus prevents the development of a universal
intellectual freedom. It results in totalitarianism (cultism) and
the implicit condonation of fraud by cover up. Consequently,
academic censors can only rank as hypocrites.
     Ethan M. Katsh (UMass/Amherst) writes in his book "The
Electronic Media and the Transformation of Law" that:

     "As the newer technologies appear on the scene, publicized
     attempts to control information will probably increase, just
     as censorship laws proliferated in the sixteenth and
     seventeenth century in the West when smuggled printed
     material interfered with local control. The inevitable end
     of the process, however, will be a realization that total
     control of public information is an anachronism."

     Parker persists in proving the anachronism although he (or
at the least his journalism department colleagues) must
comprehend that his efforts can only fail. Consequently, this
edition of CONTRA CABAL reaches carr-l subscribers as the result
of "electronic smuggling" and proves Dr. Katsh's point!

